Architecture plan for Issue #156: Configure Axum body limit for admin multipart uploads and improve errors

Current architecture
- Frontend: Next.js App Router under app/ with admin post editor form in app/admin/posts/_components/post-editor-form.tsx. The form submits multipart/form-data directly to /api/admin/posts and /api/admin/posts/:id/update.
- Backend: Rust Axum API under rust-backend/src. rust-backend/src/main.rs builds the router and registers public/admin routes. rust-backend/src/posts/admin.rs parses multipart admin post forms, validates individual image sizes and types, uploads images through rust-backend/src/storage/mod.rs, and redirects back to the admin UI.
- Persistence/storage: PostgreSQL via SQLx and S3-compatible private object storage with relative keys plus OBJECT_STORAGE_PREFIX in the storage wrapper.

Implementation plan
- Apply the global Axum request body limit in rust-backend/src/main.rs by importing/using axum::extract::DefaultBodyLimit and layering DefaultBodyLimit::max(50 * 1024 * 1024) on the router. This aligns backend acceptance with the existing frontend combined-upload warning ceiling.
- Improve multipart rejection observability/user-facing errors near the admin multipart parsing boundary in rust-backend/src/posts/admin.rs. Preserve existing per-file MAX_IMAGE_BYTES validation, but distinguish body-limit/multipart extraction failures from generic malformed forms where possible so oversize combined submissions return a helpful 400 message instead of only "Invalid multipart form."
- Keep shared limits consistent: the frontend warning currently documents 50 MB combined upload limit; backend route-level limit should use the same numeric value. The per-image 10 MB limit remains in posts/admin.rs.

Dependencies and sequencing
- Single issue only; no cross-issue dependency.
- Backend limit should be added before testing multipart behavior.
- Tests should cover router/body-limit configuration or error mapping where practical, plus existing npm/Rust tests.

Validation
- Run rust-backend unit tests with cargo test --manifest-path rust-backend/Cargo.toml.
- Run npm test for TypeScript helpers if frontend strings or shared validation are touched.
- Run npm run build if changes affect routing or backend startup.
